Again, only the sages and the Brahmins. Swanubhutamasheśena rajanbhriguvarnayat. 13. Meaning of the word:
Again—then; Cha—and; Satram—up to the Yagya; Aavrajya—having gone; Muninam—of the sages; Brahma-vadinam—skilled in the knowledge of the Vedas;
Swa—by oneself; Anubhutam—having been experienced; Asheshena—completely; Rajan—Oh King (Parikshit); Bhrigu—Bhrigu;
Avarnayat—was described. Hey Rajan, then Bhrigu returned to the Vedic scholars' sacrificial hall and told them all his
experience. Tannishtyam yat munayah, vismitah, muktaschanshayah. He who is the cause of peace and fearlessness, who is the cause of the universe. 1>. Dharmaṇaṃ, sakṣṭāya, yākaṃ, jñānaṃ, vairagyam cha tatanvitam. Possessing splendor in eightfold forms, by which the self is cleansed. 15. Of the sages, of the cremated, of the wise, of the peaceful. Those who are not attached to the saints, they say, is the supreme goal. 16. He whose essence is dear, the form is Brahman, the deities are the gods.
Bhajantayanashishah Shanta yam va nipunabuddhayah. 17. Meaning of the word

Tat—This; Nishamya—Having heard; Ath—Then; Munayah—The group of sages; Vismitaah—Astonished; Mukta—Without; Sanshayah—With doubts;
Bhuyansam—As the greatest; Shraddadhughah—He expressed faith; Vishnu—In Vishnu; Yatah—From whom; Shanti—Peace;
Yatah—From whom; Abhayam—Fearlessness; Dharmah—Dharma; Sakshat—In direct form; Yatah—From whom; Gyanam—Knowledge; Vairagyam—Detachment;
Cha—And; Tat—This (knowledge); Anvitam—Along with; Aishwaryam—Yoga power (obtained through yoga practice); Cha—And; Ashtadha—Eight
types; Yasmat—From whom; Yashah—Their fame; Cha—Also; Atma—Of the mind "Mal—impurity; Apaham—remover;
Muninam—of the sages; Nyasta—those who have renounced;" Abandoned; of the fierce; of peace; of the peaceful; Equal;
Of the conscious; of the mind; Without selfishness; Of the saints; of the saintly nature; Yam—to whom; Aahu—they say;
Supreme—supreme; Goal—aim; Sattva—quality of goodness; Yasya—whose; Priya—beloved; Murti—form; Brahmana—Brahmin;
Tu—and; Ishta—worshiped; Devatah—deities; Bhajanti—they worship; Anashishah—without any desire; Shantah—peaceful; Yam—to whom; Va—indeed; Nipuna—skilled; Buddhayah—whose intellectual capabilities. Hearing the description of Bhrigu, all the doubts of the sages were cleared, and they were assured
that Vishnu is the greatest God. From them comes peace, fearlessness, the essential principles of religion, knowledge,
Along with detachment, all eight powers of yoga, and their glory that washes away all the impurities of the mind
is attained. They go to the supreme destination of those sages who are calm, balanced, selfless, and skilled, who know.
They go, who have renounced all violence. Their most beloved form is pure, full of essence.
And Brahmins are their revered deities. People with sharp intellect, who have attained spiritual peace,
worship them with selfless devotion.
